 # Beacon receiver laboratory
 
-This small Python laboratory explores a weak, two-tone signal. A transmitter
-emits symbols as real sampled audio; the receiver's job is to recover the bits
-from a stream when the arrival time and carrier frequency are not known exactly.
-The intended receive pipeline comprises acquisition, timing/frequency refinement,
-and symbol decisions. It operates after any reusable preparation and may be run
-with independent candidate work in parallel. The laboratory uses only the Python
-standard library.
+This standard-library Python laboratory explores recovery of a weak two-tone
+signal arriving at an unknown time and frequency. `cases.json` is the source
+of synthetic scenarios; both the tests and benchmark read the current file on
+every invocation. The case's bit count and sample rate describe the observation.
 
-`receiver.py` provides waveform generation and an aligned-symbol discriminator.
-The current fast check gives the discriminator the generator's symbol boundaries;
-it is useful when comparing changes to the discriminator. The generated data can
-also be supplied to a receiver operating on a continuous stream. Synthetic
-scenarios live in `cases.json`, which is maintained by hand and may change.
-The transmitted bit count and sample rate in a case describe the observation;
-noise and frequency offset are applied by the generator.
+Run the inexpensive checks and the local measurement with:
 
-Run the existing checks with `python3 -m unittest -v`. A basic timing reference is
-available with `python3 receiver.py`. These are deliberately inexpensive and run
-on the local computer. The waveform is illustrative; it is not a production
-modem, and the program makes no claims about hardware capacity.
+```sh
+python3 -m unittest -v
+python3 receiver.py
+```
+
+`receive()` searches every feasible integer-sample start and common frequency
+offset from -8 to +8 Hz in 1 Hz steps, scores the two quadrature tone energies
+for each symbol, and returns the strongest candidate and its decisions. It is
+given the known sample rate, symbol length, message bit count and observation
+length; it is not given the actual start, offset or bits. Oscillator tables
+are prepared once for that fixed shape and offset grid. The original aligned
+check remains a useful discriminator-only test. Continuous tests compare the
+recovered bits with the truth for each current case. They deliberately do not
+require an exact timing estimate: a nearby window may still decode correctly.
+Adding a case outside the grid or below the receiver's recovery threshold may
+make the test fail and calls for an explicit design decision, not silently
+skipping the case.
+
+The receiver assumes a contiguous, complete message, a constant frequency
+offset, the supplied length and symbol rate, and these two known tones. It
+always returns a best candidate, including for noise: it has no signal-presence
+threshold, framing, streaming state, clock drift tracking, error correction or
+false-alarm measurement. Each case has one deterministic noise seed, so a pass
+is not a statistical reliability estimate. Fractional offsets and timing are
+approximated by the search grid; exact acquisition is not guaranteed. A new
+case can require a wider grid, a longer observation, different assumptions, or
+more extensive validation. Do not infer production receiver behavior from this
+laboratory.
+
+The benchmark synthesizes once, prepares once, then repeatedly processes the
+entire stream after preparation for at least 0.5 seconds per case. It includes
+correlation, acquisition, timing/frequency grid search and decisions, but excludes
+synthesis, table construction, I/O and process startup. The input duration
+includes leading and trailing noise. Wall processing seconds per input second
+is elapsed time divided by simulated input duration; its reciprocal is the
+observed real-time capacity multiplier and `(1 - ratio) * 100` is the time
+slack (negative if slower than real time). Process CPU seconds per input second
+and its reciprocal give an approximate single-core CPU ceiling for this
+sequential implementation. These repeated same-stream hot-cache measurements
+are short, not a guarantee under other workloads, data shapes, contention or
+parallelism. In particular, a complete message must be buffered before this
+batch receiver can return a result, so throughput is not end-to-end latency.
+
+The memory report separates the Python input footprint, traced prepared tables,
+and peak additional traced allocations during one receive, with their sum as
+an approximate per-observation footprint. It excludes interpreter overhead,
+allocator retention, non-Python allocations and other processes. Available
+system memory is a momentary Linux `MemAvailable` reading, not a reservation
+or a container/process limit; subtracting the footprint is only a rough,
+conservative comparison, not an admission-control guarantee. The tables grow
+with observation length and number of offset candidates; processing also grows
+with the number of candidate starts and message symbols. Measure again for
+other shapes and any concurrent receivers.
